Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever an order is refunded, in full or in part, no points are taken back from any account. The customer keeps all the points the order earned, and no deduction or reversal of any kind is made. This also holds for a friend's refunded first order: Ritu keeps the 100 points she was credited. No referral bonus rule or feature is built.
  2. Whenever a customer has points, those points never expire, however long ago they were earned or however long it has been since the customer's last order.
  3. Whenever a refund changes a customer's spending, nothing about a membership level changes. The shop has no member levels, so level rules (such as Silver at ₹10,000 spent in the year) do not apply and are not built.
  4. Whenever a customer asks to spend points, for example to take ₹300 off the next order, decline the request. Points cannot be spent. Make no one-off exception and do not convert points into store credit.
  5. Whenever an order is placed, work out the points as follows. If any part of the order was paid with store credit, it earns zero points, however the total is split between store credit and other payment methods. Otherwise, if the amount paid for the items after the discount code is applied is under ₹500, it earns nothing. Otherwise it earns one point for every full ₹50 of that amount.
  6. Whenever a refund time limit has to be counted, count the days from the day the courier marked the parcel as delivered, even if the customer says it never arrived. Never count from the order date.
  7. Whenever a refund is requested, judge it only under the current refund rules, counting days since delivery. The order date is not used to choose which rules apply. For example, Pooja's ₹2,000 dress ordered on 10 October and delivered after the rule change is judged under the new rules.
  8. Whenever goods are damaged and the customer asks for a refund within 90 days of delivery, refund the full amount.
  9. Whenever a refund is requested for any reason other than damage (such as a change of mind or late delivery, which is not a reason in the policy), the refund depends on the days since delivery. From day 1 to day 7 (day 7 included) it is the full amount. From day 8 to day 30 it is half the amount. After day 30 it is nothing. For example, a request at day 10 gets half.
  10. Whenever an item is refunded, the refund amount is based on what the customer actually paid. If the item was part of a free-item offer (a ₹1,000 shirt with a ₹300 belt free, shirt returned and belt kept), the base is the ₹1,000 paid, and the belt's ₹300 is not taken off. If the order had a percentage discount, the base is the item's proportional share of the amount paid after that discount (₹500 × 0.9 = ₹450 for a scarf), even if the remaining items alone would not have qualified for the code. The day-based fraction is then applied to this base.
  11. Whenever an item is custom-made (such as a blouse stitched to the customer's measurements), treat it like any other item for returns. If it is not damaged, the standard refund schedule and refund amount rules apply (for example ₹1,800 refunded in full for a ₹1,800 blouse returned on day 3).
  12. Whenever a customer returns an item for change of mind within 7 days of delivery, the customer pays the return courier charge (e.g. ₹150) and it is not deducted from the refund. The refund is the item's full price (e.g. ₹3,000 for the jacket).
  13. Whenever a refund is calculated, the shipping fee is not refunded, except for damaged goods.
  14. Whenever a refund to an order's original card is rejected by the bank because the card has been closed, still refund to a card or bank account, never as store credit (money goes back the way it was paid, per policy rule 8). Ask the customer for a working card or bank details and send the refund there. Do not hold the refund indefinitely.
  15. Whenever a refund follows the policy's rules and is requested within 10 days of delivery, pay it automatically with no approval step. For example, a ₹12,000 refund request at day 10 is refunded ₹6,000, paid automatically with no one approving it.
